  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is <b>YAML configuration files kept in Amazon S3 buckets</b>. AWS QuickSight can analyze a wide range of data sources, including Amazon Redshift (<i>Amazon Redshift clusters hosted within your AWS account</i>), on-premises and internet-accessible MySQL databases (<i>A MySQL 5.1 database located in your company's data center and accessible over the Internet</i>), and all supported versions of Amazon RDS like MariaDB (<i>An RDS instance running MariaDB</i>). However, QuickSight can only process well-structured tabular data formats such as CSV, TSV, CLF, ELF, and JSON when fetching data from S3, not YAML files. YAML isn't recognized as a structured datasource for QuickSight; you would need to transform this data into a compatible format before using it.<br><br><b>Tip:</b> Always verify that your data is in a QuickSight-supported format before attempting to connect. Understanding data format limitations is crucial when setting up or automating reporting and analytics pipelines on AWS.</p>",
  "query": "<p>As a DevOps engineer for a major supermarket chain, you've been tasked with deploying AWS QuickSight to analyze large volumes of data from both AWS and on-premises environments. This data contains items like daily transactions and customer information. <br>To create reports, explore hidden patterns using machine learning, and build dashboards, which one of the following data sources is <b>not</b> accessible directly to QuickSight for analysis?</p>",
